1 Introduction

In statistical learning theory, there is generally a fundamental assumption that the training samples and test samples should be located in the same distribution, i.e. independent identical distribution (IID) (Liu et al. 2023; Xiong et al. 2022; Zhu et al. 2022). In this way, the learned classification model from the training samples can be utilized to well classify the test samples (Zhang et al. 2021). However, it is usually impossible to guarantee that the feature distribution of the training and test samples is always the same in many applications due to the illumination, pose, environment, resolution, background, which can make the above assumption untenable. That is to say, if the distributions of both the training samples and test samples are not identical, the model obtained by the training samples usually fails. Figure 1 gives several sample images with same semantics information but different feature distribution, in which there are four data sets (each column is correspond to a data set), and they have the different distribution, but each dataset includes the same objects. It is easy to understand that if any one dataset is utilized to train a classification model, the obtained classifier will not work well when it is used to classify other data sets.
For overcoming the problem mentioned above, a simple and effective method is to collect a great many of data samples for training set, which includes as many diverse distribution as possible and can well characterize the feature distribution of the test sample set. However, it spends a lot of time and labor to collect the sufficient labeling sample data. Consequently, how to reduce domain mismatch by means of another semantic related but different distribution domain is becoming an interesting issue. Recently, because of no limitation of IID assumption, transfer learning is an effective and feasible tool to address the problem by making the learned knowledge or information from the training samples (source domain) apply to the test samples (target domain), and it is paid close attention increasingly. Many works for transfer learning have been presented in different fields, such as object classification and recognition (Lu et al. 2023; Zhang et al. 2016, 2022), image retrieval (Xu et al. 2022; Kan et al. 2021) and motion segmentation (Wang et al. 2019; Zhao et al. 2022), which have achieved good performance.
Domain adaptation is a widely studied technology of transfer learning, in which the problem of distribution disparity can be solved, and the classification discriminative

