ability is transferred to the unlabeled target domain samples by means of using the samples of the labeled source domain at the same time. Therefore, it attracts wide attention in recent years. A generalized transfer subspace learning (GTSL) method (Shao et al. 2014) is proposed by utilizing the low-rank constraint. In GTSL, both the samples from the source and target domain samples are respectively projected into a generalized lowdimensional feature space, where any sample from target domain is linearly represented by the samples from the source domain. In order to reduce the domain distribution shift, Jhuo et al. (2012) present a low-rank reconstruction based robust domain adaptation algorithm. It is well known that the local geometry structure of the samples usually plays a significant part in reducing the local domain mismatch. By utilizing the domain locality, Zhang et al. (2019) present a transfer learning algorithm based on manifold criterion. In order to learn the useful representation, a stacked adaptive regularization based auto-regressions method (Jiang et al. 2019) is proposed for domain adaptation problems. Liu et al. (2021) present a unified cross-domain recognition algorithm based on statistical and geometric adaptations, in which the classifier construction and the divergence minimization are modeled in a unified way. Liu et al. (2020) present a geodesic flow kernel model based on Grassmann-linear monotonic maps for heterogeneous unsupervised domain adaptation (UDA). However, the label information is not fully exploited in the above methods, which helps to improve the recognition performance. Based on this, some methods are presented. A discriminative transfer subspace learning algorithm (Xu et al. 2016) is presented, in which a nonnegative label relaxation matrix is obtained to reduce the domain discrepancy. Chang et al. (2020) propose a unified domain adaptation framework, in which the statistical and geometric characteristics are incorporated. In order to find the invariant features, an explicit feature based domain adaptation algorithm (Deng et al. 2019) is presented for feature selection. For migrating the local geometry structure, Lu et al. (2021) present a manifold learning based transfer method via discriminative regression analysis, in which the discriminative information from the source domain is encoded to the target domain by means of between-class graph and within-class graph. There exists another problem for these methods as follows. A same projected matrix is shared to respectively migrate the source samples and target samples to the common low-dimensional space, where the difference of samples from the source and target domain may not be effectively measured.
Based on the above observations, we present a double projection and relaxed label based domain adaptation method termed manifold transfer subspace learning based on double relaxed discriminative regression (MTSL-DRDR). Firstly, both the between-class graph and within-class graph are introduced, and the discriminative information from the source domain is encoded to the target domain. Secondly, the local neighborhood relation of the samples with the similar semantics from the two domains is preserved by means of the adaptive weight graph. Thirdly, instead of utilizing a single projection matrix, two transformation matrices are introduced to alleviate the over-fitting problem. The main idea of our method is shown in Fig. 2. The key work and innovations of the paper are given as follows.
(1) The discriminative projection, manifold learning, low-rank representation, and relaxed regression are integrated into a united framework for unsupervised domain adaptation, and a discriminant and common subspace is obtained, where the distribution difference of the two domains can be effectively alleviated.
(2) In the common projection subspace, the samples with the same semantics from the source domain and target domain are interlaced.

