3.3 Complexity and convergence analysis of the proposed method
(1) Computation complexity: In Algorithm 1, the time-consuming steps mainly include steps 1, 2, 5 and 6, in which the multiplication operations, matrix inversion and the singular value decomposition operation are contained. Specifically, the computational complexities for Steps 1 and 2 are r e s p e c t i v e l y O(d2(ns + nt) + d2c + dns(ns + nt) + d3 + d(ns + nt)c) a n d O(d2(ns + nt) + d2c + dnt(ns + nt) + d3 + d(ns + nt)c) . In Step 5, the main time complexity is about O(d(ns + nt)c + nsc(ns + nt) + n3s + n2s nt) . The computing complexity in Step 6 is O((ns + nt)3) . In a word, the total time complexity for Algorithm 1 is approximate O( (d3 + d2(ns + nt) + dns(ns + nt) + (ns + nt)3) , where denotes the iterative number.
(2) Convergence Analysis: Though the convergence of the exact ALM method is proved in Bertsekas (1982), the convergence of IALM is difficult to be proved when it includes three or more variables (our method includes seven variables) in theory. Fortunately, if three sufficient conditions are satisfied, the convergence can be achieved (Eckstein and Bertsekas 1992).
A. The full column rank should be satisfied for the dictionary matrix (in Algorithm 1, Xs itself is used to be dictionary matrix);
B. The obtained value in the optimal problem (16) should be monotonically decreasing each iteration.
C. The parameter in Algorithm 1 exists upper bounded.
The above three conditions are usually satisfied. Therefore, the proposed algorithm is convergent. Meanwhile, In Sect. 4, the convergence of our algorithm is experimentally validated, and the convergence curve of the algorithm also proves it.

