4 Experiments

In this subsection, some experiments are performed to validate the superiority and effectiveness of MTSL-RRDR algorithm on several challenging benchmark datasets. Specifically, these databases mainly include three categories: (1) cross-domain object recognition, such as MSRC + VOC (Long et al. 2014), COIL-100 databases (Long et al. 2014) and Office + Caltech-256 (Long et al. 2013); (2) cross-pose face classification: CMU PIE (Long et al. 2014); (3) cross-illumination face recognition: the extended Yale B (Shao et al. 2014). A group of popular methods are utilized for comparison, such as, transfer subspace learning (TSL) (Si et al. 2010), low-rank TSL (LTSL) (Shao et al. 2014), joint distribution adaptation (JDA) (Long et al. 2013), transfer component analysis (TCA) (Pan et al. 2011), latent domain adaptation depression recognition based on low rank (LDADR) (Tao et al. 2019), stacked robust adaptively regularized auto-regression (SRARAs) (Jiang et al. 2019), DTS (Xu et al. 2016), MTL-DRA (Lu et al. 2021), and the classical PCA method (Belhumeur et al. 1997). In addition, the parameter values in the compared methods are referenced the original papers.

4.1 Experiments on the Office and Caltech‑256 image datasets
The Office and Caltech-256 data sets are widely used cross-domain image databases. The Office image database contains three sub datasets with a total of 4652 images, and three image subsets are respectively Amazon (A), DSLR (D), and Webcam (W), which respectively includes 31 different objects, such as, laptop, keyboard, bike, and so on. There are totally 30,607 images from 256 classes in Caltech-256 (C). There are ten same classes in the four datasets. Several example images from the ten objects are illustrated in Fig. 3. In the experiment, any two data subsets are randomly chosen to respectively act as the source domain and target domain, and the total of 12 pairs of image sets, such as, W→D, W→A, W→C, …, C→A, are available for experiments. The experiment results of all methods are illustrated in Fig. 4. It can be seen from Fig. 4 that the proposed method can obtain the best classification performance on the 12 crossdomain data sets.

4.2 Experiments on MSRC and VOC 2007 data sets
There are 4323 sample images from 18 different objects in the MSRC database, and VOC 2007 image database has 5011 sample images from 20 objects. There are six same semantic classes in two datasets including car, sheep, cow, bird, airplane and bicycle, which are shown in Fig. 5. According to the reference (Long et al. 2014), two crossdomain data sets are constructed. One is MSRC vs VOC 2007 (M→V) dataset, in which the 1269 samples of MSRC database are utilized to be the source domain, and the 1530 data samples of VOC 2007 are utilized for the target domain. The other is VOC 2007 vs MSRC (V→M) datase, in which the above two databases are switched. The sample images are resized to 256 pixels, and the 128-dimensional dense SIFT features are extracted by VLFeat open source package. Finally, the 240-dimensional codebook corresponding to each image is obtained by utilizing K-means clustering method.
