4.6 Classification result discussion

Through the observation of the above experiment results, we draw the following conclusions.
(1) Since there are no limits of IID, most methods based on transfer learning have better performance than the non transfer learning methods.
(2) Our MTSL-DRDR performs better than the discriminative regression based algorithms, such as, DTSL and MTL-DRA. The proposed method indeed provides more freedom by utilizing double transformation matrices, in which the problem of over-fitting can be solved and better margins is achieved. In another way, it is very important to relieve the over-fitting problem for improving the recognition accuracies.
(3) The proposed MTSL-DRDR achieves the best performance among all comparison algorithms in most case. That is because the local discriminative information from the source domain can be transferred into the target domain by MTSL-DRDR, and it effectively solves the over-fitting problem.
(4) Though some low-rank representation based transfer learning algorithms obtain good classification precision, our method is still superior to the comparison algorithms. The reason is that comparison algorithms only find the low-rank or sparse representation for data reconstruction. For obvious reasons, the good data reconstruction does not mean the good discriminative power.

